# Simple human language

Write like a person talking to a room, not like a blog post or AI draft.

## Never use em dashes

Do not use em dashes (`—`) or HTML `&mdash;` anywhere in slide copy, speaker notes, or comments.

Use a period, comma, colon, or a short new sentence instead.

```markdown
❌ Great at explaining and drafting—blind to your repo.
✅ Great at explaining and drafting. Blind to your repo.

❌ Plus SQL—because almost every app stores data.
✅ Plus SQL, because almost every app stores data.

❌ They didn't disappear — they became the foundation.
✅ They didn't disappear. They became the foundation.
```

En dashes in number or year ranges are fine (`350 – 550`, `2017–2019`, `30–40 sec`).

## Keep the language human and simple

- Short sentences. One idea per sentence when you can.
- Everyday words over buzzwords. If a term is required, define it in plain words right away.
- Sound like you are speaking out loud, not writing an essay.
- Prefer "you / we / it" over abstract phrasing.
- Cut filler and stacked clauses. If a sentence needs an em dash to breathe, rewrite it as two sentences.
